1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,740
Welcome to the Smart Electricity Load Management System, a Design and Analysis of Algorithms mini project. It simulates how

2
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:13,081
limited electricity can be distributed among areas with different demands and priorities. This walkthrough shows the currently deployed, browser

3
00:00:13,081 --> 00:00:19,421
based application.

4
00:00:20,421 --> 00:00:27,086
The current VOLT GRID dashboard uses HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and bundled Chart.js. Algorithms execute in your browser, without a

5
00:00:27,086 --> 00:00:33,752
Python backend or database. Records and reports are saved in local storage. Each browser and website address has its

6
00:00:33,752 --> 00:00:40,417
own data.

7
00:00:41,417 --> 00:00:47,998
The overview combines eight summary cards with four charts. You can inspect available capacity, total demand, allocated power, remaining

8
00:00:47,998 --> 00:00:54,578
capacity, area counts, and utilization. The charts compare supply with demand and show area demands, unused capacity, and distribution

9
00:00:54,578 --> 00:01:01,158
by priority.

10
00:01:02,158 --> 00:01:09,403
The demand registry lets you add, edit, and delete areas. Each record has a unique identifier, name, demand in

11
00:01:09,403 --> 00:01:16,649
kilowatts, priority category, and area type. The web app supports up to one hundred areas. Priority benefits are Critical

12
00:01:16,649 --> 00:01:23,894
one hundred, High seventy, Medium forty, and Low twenty.

13
00:01:24,894 --> 00:01:31,609
Available capacity must be a whole number from zero to five thousand kilowatts. Area demand must be positive. Each

14
00:01:31,609 --> 00:01:38,325
load receives its full requested power or none at all. Changing records or capacity clears the current allocation, while

15
00:01:38,325 --> 00:01:45,040
previously saved report snapshots remain unchanged.

16
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:52,910
Greedy first uses manual Merge Sort to order areas by descending priority benefit, then ascending identifier. It accepts each

17
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:59,781
whole load that fits and skips loads that do not. This is a fast priority policy, but it does

18
00:01:59,781 --> 00:02:06,651
not guarantee the best combined benefit. Recorded steps show every decision.

19
00:02:07,651 --> 00:02:15,621
Zero one Knapsack treats demand as weight and priority benefit as value. A dynamic programming matrix evaluates capacities, and

20
00:02:15,621 --> 00:02:23,591
traceback reconstructs the selected areas. It maximizes total benefit under the stated capacity model. Its work and storage grow

21
00:02:23,591 --> 00:02:31,561
with both area count and numeric capacity. Unused power can remain.

22
00:02:32,561 --> 00:02:41,170
For the current six area demo at six hundred kilowatts, both standalone allocators select the hospital, Residential Area A,

23
00:02:41,170 --> 00:02:49,778
Government School, and Residential Area B. They allocate five hundred and eighty kilowatts, leave twenty unused, supply four of

24
00:02:49,778 --> 00:02:58,386
six areas, and achieve benefit two hundred and eighty. One shared outcome does not prove Greedy is always optimal.

25
00:02:59,386 --> 00:03:06,805
Merge Sort recursively splits records into halves, sorts each half, and merges them. The lab can rank demand in

26
00:03:06,805 --> 00:03:14,223
ascending or descending order, using the area identifier to break ties. Its conventional time complexity is n log n.

27
00:03:14,223 --> 00:03:21,642
It also prepares priority order for Greedy and identifier order for searching.

28
00:03:22,642 --> 00:03:30,285
Binary Search repeatedly inspects the midpoint of an identifier sorted copy and discards half the remaining range. The search

29
00:03:30,285 --> 00:03:37,929
core takes logarithmic time. The application measures the fresh identifier sort separately, because preparation makes a complete lookup more

30
00:03:37,929 --> 00:03:45,572
expensive than the search alone. Here, identifier three finds the Industrial Zone.

31
00:03:46,572 --> 00:03:54,836
The Performance page compares selected algorithms on one input snapshot, showing operation counts, complexity, output, and timing. Sorting, searching,

32
00:03:54,836 --> 00:04:03,099
and allocating solve different problems. A smaller runtime therefore does not make one algorithm a better replacement for another.

33
00:04:03,099 --> 00:04:11,362
Allocation quality must also consider the achieved benefit and capacity feasibility.

34
00:04:12,362 --> 00:04:20,417
The Algorithm Report runs only when you explicitly request it. Choose current or representative inputs, measured runs, warmups, seed,

35
00:04:20,417 --> 00:04:28,472
and trace mode. It reports minimum, average, median, maximum, and ninety fifth percentile runtime in milliseconds, together with the

36
00:04:28,472 --> 00:04:36,527
environment, collection time, source functions, and output checks.

37
00:04:37,527 --> 00:04:46,944
The runtime chart shows median and ninety fifth percentile values. Core execution, identifier sort preparation, and complete compute call

38
00:04:46,944 --> 00:04:56,360
time are separate scopes. Rendering, storage, downloads, and network requests are outside the timed compute calls. Export CSV for

39
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:05,777
analysis or JSON for raw samples. No actual memory usage or prediction accuracy is claimed.

40
00:05:06,777 --> 00:05:14,122
Each allocation saves a report snapshot containing the input areas, capacity, selected and unselected loads, benefit, utilization, and timing.

41
00:05:14,122 --> 00:05:21,467
You can inspect historical results and download CSV. The browser retains up to one hundred reports. Export anything you

42
00:05:21,467 --> 00:05:28,812
need before clearing browser storage.

43
00:05:29,812 --> 00:05:37,683
The header offers four dark and four light themes. Color choices update charts, forms, reports, and three dimensional accents,

44
00:05:37,683 --> 00:05:45,553
and the preference is remembered. The layout adapts to smaller screens. The illustrated city is decorative: it does not

45
00:05:45,553 --> 00:05:53,423
calculate physical electricity routes, voltage, or transmission losses.

46
00:05:54,423 --> 00:06:02,851
The original Python and Tkinter edition is also retained in the repository. It uses integer priorities, essential flags, and

47
00:06:02,851 --> 00:06:11,280
capacity up to ten thousand kilowatts. Greedy serves essential loads first, then Knapsack selects optional loads. That hybrid is

48
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:19,708
not globally optimal across all loads. The current web edition runs the two allocators independently.

49
00:06:20,708 --> 00:06:29,184
The project is publicly deployed on Render and Vercel, with source on GitHub. The personalized Render address is shown

50
00:06:29,184 --> 00:06:37,661
here. It needs no account to open and no database server. This is an educational simulation, with no demand

51
00:06:37,661 --> 00:06:46,138
prediction, machine learning, time slot scheduler, or physical switching. Explore the labs and run your own performance report.
